Would it also make sense to extend this to arrays in the future? For example, if you wanted to search for UTF-8 substrings:
```cs
private static readonly IndexOfAnyValues<byte[]> s_names = IndexOfAnyValues.Create(
    new[] { "Sherlock"u8.ToArray(), "Holmes"u8.ToArray(), "Watson"u8.ToArray() });

public static int CountNames(ReadOnlySpan<byte> text)
{
    int count = 0;

    while (!text.IsEmpty)
    {
        int matchOffset = text.IndexOfAny(s_names);

        if ((uint)matchOffset >= (uint)text.Length) break;

        int matchLength = text[matchOffset] == 'S' ? 8 : 6;

        text = text.Slice(matchOffset + matchLength);
        count++;
    }

    return count;
}
```
Maybe `byte[]` isn't the best fit, but I think it's valuable to have UTF-8 support in some form here.

[Video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tfCkoEqoVgI&t=1h31m56s)

We had a long discussion about the implications of just returning the `int` match and felt it was OK (we could extend it later to out a match length or the matching term).

Looks good as proposed.

```C#
namespace System.Buffers;

public static partial class IndexOfAnyValues
{
    public static IndexOfAnyValues<string> Create(ReadOnlySpan<string> values, StringComparison comparisonType);
}

namespace System;

public static partial class MemoryExtensions
{
    public static int IndexOfAny(this ReadOnlySpan<char> span, IndexOfAnyValues<string> values);

    // From https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/86528
    public static bool ContainsAny(this ReadOnlySpan<char> span, SearchValues<string> values);
}
```

@MihaZupan related, is there/was there a proposal for `string.ContainsAny(char[])` ? 

eg., for use places like this which currently have to use IndexOf. https://github.com/dotnet/msbuild/blob/4ffba3fe0dd35a30cc892bc8c202a006acb8f20a/src/Build/Evaluation/Expander.cs#L401

IIRC, we have some optimizations for Contains that can make it faster than IndexOf.

review_comments:
> Can we just say we don't use this implementation if any of the strings being searched for contains surrogates

Is it a guarantee that a surrogate pair under `IgnoreCase` will never match a non-surrogate input character?

> And why do we need different implementations for ICU vs NLS?

With ICU and Invariant, we have fast single-character or single-surrogate-pair case conversions available (`Ordinal.ToUpper`, `InvariantModeCasing.ToUpper`, `SurrogateCasing.ToUpper`).
With NLS, we have to [go through interop](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/112be1b9d055b755e5ee1b27d90c29df8cad7e6e/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Globalization/TextInfo.Nls.cs#L28). I can check just how much more expensive doing that for every character would be. All `IndexOfAnyCaseInsensitiveUnicodeNls` is doing is trying to minimize that cost.

> Can we just say we don't use this implementation
> I wouldn't think it's worth writing any new code for NLS at all.

We need _some_ code (we need _a_ multi-value fallback at some point - right now this is it).
But we could go with a simpler (and NotFast:tm:) approach. This would be the most basic implementation we could use:
```c#
bool IndexOfAny(ReadOnlySpan<char> text, string[] values)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < text.Length; i++)
    {
        foreach (string value in values)
        {
            if (text.Slice(i).StartsWith(value, OrdinalIgnoreCase))
                return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

If we're saying we don't care about NLS perf, this is fine.

Doing the same for anything that contains surrogates would be a huge perf cliff though.
